# ---------- 1. EMERGENCY FUNDS ----------
body1 = f"""
<p>No story on this page starts with investing. Every one of them starts with a buffer, a boring pile of cash sitting exactly where it can be reached, waiting for the boiler to break or the contract to end. The creators disagree about plenty, funds and pensions and property, but on this they speak with one voice. Before a single pound goes near the stock market, three to six months of essential spending must sit somewhere safe. What differs is how they learned it, how much they keep, and what the money bought them beyond safety.</p>
<p>The sharpest version comes from {qm("mia")}, whose video on financially preparing to move out has been watched more than 58,000 times. She lays down the rule before anything about rent or bills, and she frames it as the price of independence itself.</p>
{qu("mia", "If you are living at home and working, I would be aiming to save at least a three month emergency fund before you decide to actually move out. An emergency fund is there in case you have an emergency, like your boiler breaks or you lose your job, and you need money to cover you while you sort it out.", "https://www.tiktok.com/@miarosemcgrath/video/7689447975675825430", "58,472 views, 5,669 likes", "Her six step move out guide has 296 shares, the most saved video in her catalogue that month.")}
<p>Mia built a multiple six figure net worth in her twenties, with 100,000 pounds invested, and she treats the emergency fund as choreography rather than caution. Save first, move second, and the freedom that follows is real because it was paid for in advance. The fund is not pessimism. It is the entry ticket to every bigger decision, and she refuses to let her viewers buy the ticket after they have already walked through the door.</p>
<p>{qm("jno")} arrives at the same rule from the opposite direction, as step two of a deliberate pre investing checklist. In a video laying out the three things he did before putting a single pound into the stock market, clearing expensive debt comes first and the buffer comes immediately after, sized with unusual precision.</p>
{qu("jno", "The first thing I did was get rid of any high interest debt, so things like credit cards, because if you are paying 20 percent interest on a credit card, you would be much better off paying that off instead of investing in something that is going to go up or down. The second thing I did was build an emergency fund. For me, I decided to save six months of expenses so that if anything happened, I would be covered.", "https://www.tiktok.com/@jno_pwnr/video/7690226697307737366", "1,592 views", "Part one of his Investing from Zero series.")}
<p>Six months rather than three, and the reasoning is pure arithmetic. A credit card charging 20 percent will devour any market return, so debt dies first. Then the fund gets built to six months of expenses, not income, a distinction the careful creators always make. JNO counts what leaving costs, not what arriving pays, and the fund covers exactly that number for exactly half a year.</p>
<p>{qm("ciara")} gives the idea its most methodical expression. In a video watched more than 33,000 times, she describes going from zero to 100,000 pounds invested in just under five years, and she names the emergency fund as the first of three things she wishes she had known, with a calculation method attached.</p>
{qu("ciara", "You need to be saving three to six months emergency fund. I know this is a lot of money and it does not sound very fun, but you need to do it. You first need to calculate your essential expenses, so think rent, mortgage, bills, things that you absolutely have to pay, and then multiply that by three or by six.", "https://www.tiktok.com/@ciara__anne/video/7686551034176589089", "33,400 views", "Her most watched money video, and the one her viewers quote back at her.")}
<p>Ciara is blunt that the number feels miserable. Nobody dreams about holding six months of rent in cash while markets rise without them. But she went from being, in her own words, pretty dumb with money to holding multiple six figures invested, and the unglamorous fund was the foundation she refused to skip. Essentials only, multiplied by three or six, held in cash, and only then does the investing conversation begin.</p>
<p>{qm("chris")} shows what the finished product looks like in the wild. He holds 5,444 pounds in savings, every pound assigned a purpose, and he walks his viewers through the architecture in a video that draws thousands of views from savers who recognise their own chaos in his before picture.</p>
{qu("chris", "I have 5,444 pounds in savings currently, and I have set up my accounts in a way that means every pound has a purpose. I am building an emergency fund of three to six months of my essential living expenses to ensure that I am protected should anything go wrong, whether that be losing my job, getting ill or anything like that.", "https://www.tiktok.com/@intentional.finance/video/7692776884307758358", "8,047 views", "He also saves monthly for annual bills like car insurance, to dodge monthly payment interest.")}
<p>Chris bought a flat at 21 with a 30,000 pound deposit, skipped university, and job hopped his way from a 19,000 pound starting salary upward, so his respect for buffers is earned rather than theoretical. His system gives each pound a job, the emergency fund guards against catastrophe, the bills pot smooths the lumpy costs, and nothing sits anywhere by accident. It is the least exciting video on this page and possibly the most useful.</p>
<p>Then {qm("amy")} renames the whole concept and reveals what it is really for. Her fund is not called an emergency fund at all. It is a fund with a ruder name and a grander purpose, and it bought her something no emergency ever could, the ability to walk away from a six figure banking job with nothing lined up.</p>
{qu("amy", "I quit my six figure job in banking without another job lined up and to do this, I spent years building a fund that buys freedom. Most people might call it an emergency fund, but I think that is a little bit too limiting for what it can do for you. Because that money is not just for emergencies. It is the money to buy you the ability to leave, whether that be a job, a relationship, or just take three months to figure out what is next.", "https://www.tiktok.com/@financiallyher_/video/7692476008464715040", "1,387 views", "She built six figures invested by 26, mostly by raising her income rather than cutting coffee.")}
<p>Where they agree, and where they split, is the question of size and purpose, and the fault lines are honest ones. Mia says three months is enough to move out on. JNO wants six months before investing a penny. Ciara offers the range and makes viewers do the multiplication themselves. Chris holds 5,444 pounds and keeps building toward the top of the range. Amy keeps the largest buffer of all, years of freedom money, because her fund insures not her boiler but her autonomy. Nobody on this page says skip it. Nobody says one month will do. The argument is only ever about whether the fund protects a life or purchases one, and the wisest answer in the room is that it does both in that order.</p>
<p>The practical synthesis fits in a single breath. Add up the spending you cannot cancel, rent or mortgage plus bills plus food plus transport, multiply by three at minimum and six for comfort, park it in an instant access account you never otherwise touch, and only then open the investing app. Every creator here did it in that order, from the 25 year old with six figures to the banker who bought her own resignation. The market will still be there when the buffer is built. The emergencies, as several of them cheerfully note, will not wait, which is exactly why the buffer comes first.</p>
"""
check_no_dashes(body1)
open("blog/emergency-funds-uk.html", "w").write(page(
 "emergency-funds-uk.html",
 "Emergency Funds: How Much, Where to Keep It, and the Fund That Buys Freedom",
 "The safety net every creator mentions before investing a penny",
 "MONEY ADVICE",
 "Emergency Funds: How Much, Where to Keep It, and the Fund That Buys Freedom",
 "The safety net every creator mentions before investing a penny",
 ["Saving"], body1))
print("wrote emergency-funds-uk.html")

# ---------- 2. KILL DEBT ----------
body2 = f"""
<p>Debt is the only topic on this site where the creators sound angry. They joke about market dips and shrug at bad months, but mention a credit card balance carried at 20 percent and the tone changes completely. Something personal has happened to each of them, a Tesco card maxed at 21, a student loan that dragged for years, a sinking fund system built precisely so it never happens again. The consensus is total and unusually emotional. Expensive debt dies before investing begins, and anyone telling you otherwise is selling something.</p>
<p>The most complete system belongs to {qm("sophie")}, who describes going from minus 33,000 pounds in student debt to multiple six figures saved and invested, and credits a single mechanism for keeping her out of debt for years while spending freely on holidays and Christmas.</p>
{qu("sophie", "One of my biggest flexes in life is the fact that I have never had credit card debt, and the fact that I do not feel guilty about spending, ever. It is all thanks to sinking funds. They are the one system that has kept me out of debt for years, even while spending freely on holidays, buying Christmas presents, and paying for life surprises.", "https://www.tiktok.com/@itssophieblank/video/7675026659027815702", "2,007 views, 78 likes", "Her payday routine video, watched alongside a breakdown of how she calculates each fund.")}
<p>Sophie started investing with 100 pounds in July 2018 with zero knowledge, paid off her student loan, and watched her net worth skyrocket afterward. Her own telling makes the sequence explicit. The loan was a financial drag for years, freedom came the month it cleared, and the sinking funds exist so she never slides back. Pots of money set aside beforehand for events and wishlist items, funded monthly, so the credit card never has to bridge a gap that planning already closed.</p>
<p>{qm("jno")} states the mathematics with the bluntness of a man who ran the numbers before risking a pound. Opening his Investing from Zero series, he puts debt clearance ahead of everything, ahead of the emergency fund, ahead of the first investment, because the comparison is not close.</p>
{qu("jno", "The first thing I did was get rid of any high interest debt, so things like credit cards. Because if you are paying 20 percent interest on a credit card, you would be much better off paying that off instead of investing in something that is going to go up or down.", "https://www.tiktok.com/@jno_pwnr/video/7690226697307737366", "1,592 views", "Steps two and three were a six month emergency fund and learning the basics first.")}
<p>There is no hedging in that sentence and none is needed. A guaranteed 20 percent return from clearing a card beats any plausible market outcome, and the market outcome is not even guaranteed. JNO treats this as the entrance exam for investing. Pass it, build the buffer, learn the basics, and only then does a pound go near a share.</p>
<p>{qm("daniel")} supplies the cautionary tale, told against himself. Looking back at 21, he names the single decision he would undo, a store card with a limit that now reads like a trap designed specifically for young men with new jobs.</p>
{qu("daniel", "If I could go back and speak to my 21 year old self, I would first of all ask him to lose some weight and keep it off. But other than that, here is the money advice I would give. Number one is do not get that Tesco credit card. They are going to give you a 6,400 pound limit and you are going to spend every single penny of it.", "https://www.tiktok.com/@gray.area.finance/video/7691300060155448598", "2,151 views", "A 31 year old father comparing his finances to UK medians in his most watched video, seen 80,400 times.")}
<p>Daniel now makes measured videos about cash ISA allowances and the state pension triple lock, but the Tesco card story is the one his viewers remember, because most of them own its modern equivalent. The limit felt like wealth at 21. It was, as he eventually learned, a high interest loan wearing a loyalty scheme as a disguise.</p>
<p>{qm("mia")} widens the frame beyond cards to the milestones that make debt clearance automatic. In a video watched more than 71,000 times, she lists the financial targets she believes every twenty something should chase, and killing expensive borrowing sits at the centre of a broader choreography of saving, investing, and pension building.</p>
{qu("mia", "These are four financial milestones I think you should be trying to hit in your 20s. This is coming from a 25 year old that has a multiple six figure net worth. And I spent my early 20s in a very intentional way, building the habits that would carry me, paying off anything expensive I owed, saving my first emergency buffer, and then investing every single month without fail.", "https://www.tiktok.com/@miarosemcgrath/video/7684166553079778582", "71,024 views, 5,501 likes", "One of the most liked videos in her catalogue, with 219 shares.")}
<p>Mia never worked in finance and did not grow up financially literate, which is precisely why her milestones land. She spent seven years reading everything before trusting herself, and the order she teaches is the order she lived. Expensive debt cleared, buffer saved, monthly investing automated, and the compounding that followed did the rest.</p>
<p>Then {qm("lupes")} walks in and complicates the sermon, deliberately. In one of his most loved clips he argues that zero percent finance, used by someone disciplined, is not debt at all but free money, and he would take it over cash almost every time.</p>
{qu("lupes", "Zero percent finance is the best thing since cooked food. To the point where I will even say if you got the option to buy something expensive in cash today or get it through zero percent finance, zero percent finance is almost always the better option. Almost, almost always. Because if you are not good with money, please do not finance anything. But if you are, then it just makes so much more sense.", "https://www.tiktok.com/@lupeslively/video/7686868008857373974", "83,500 views", "His caveat is doing heavy lifting: knowing that one pound today is worth more than one pound tomorrow only helps if the balance clears on time.")}
<p>This is where the creators split, and the split is genuinely instructive. Sophie, JNO, Daniel, and Mia treat consumer borrowing as the enemy, full stop, because they have seen what it does to people who cannot clear it monthly. Lupes agrees about anything charging interest, then carves out a precise exception for interest free credit used by people with systems, arguing that keeping cash invested while paying over 24 months at zero percent is simple arithmetic. The weight of voices sits firmly with the absolutists, and Lupes himself concedes the point with his caveat. If you are not good with money, do not finance anything. Most people, most of the time, are safer acting as though he never made the exception.</p>
<p>The takeaway survives the disagreement intact. List every balance charging interest, attack the highest rate first while paying minimums elsewhere, automate sinking funds for the predictable lumps like Christmas and car repairs so new debt never starts, and only then begin investing. Sophie cleared 33,000 pounds of student drag and built six figures. Daniel still winces at a supermarket card from a decade ago. The market returns seven percent in a good year. Your card charges twenty in every year. Pay the card.</p>
"""
check_no_dashes(body2)
open("blog/kill-debt-first.html", "w").write(page(
 "kill-debt-first.html",
 "Kill High-Interest Debt Before You Invest: The Creators' Consensus",
 "Credit cards, Klarna and student loans, what to clear first and why",
 "MONEY ADVICE",
 "Kill High-Interest Debt Before You Invest: The Creators' Consensus",
 "Credit cards, Klarna and student loans, what to clear first and why",
 ["Debt"], body2))
