# ---------- 3. PENSION BASICS ----------
body3 = f"""
<p>The pension is the strangest object in British personal finance. It is a pay rise most workers refuse to collect, a tax refund most savers never claim, and a million pound outcome most twenty somethings cannot picture. The creators on this page have one job, to make the invisible visible, and they do it with numbers from their own accounts. A 37,000 pound pot aimed at 100,000 by 30. A 140,000 pound pension engine inside a 400,000 pound net worth. Forty five percent of British adults saving nothing at all. The gap between those sentences is the entire subject of this post.</p>
<p>The alarm is sounded by {qm("james")}, whose pension videos carry the starkest statistics on this page. He does not open with his own success but with a national failure, stated flatly, in a clip that has drawn thousands of views from people who recognised themselves in it.</p>
{qu("james", "45 percent of British adults are not saving into a pension at all. 96 percent of fully self employed people are not saving for retirement in any form. If any of these sound like you, now is the time to make a change, because every year you wait is compounding you will never get back.", "https://www.tiktok.com/@moneystocker/video/7689575356805713175", "3,070 views", "James documents his own 400,000 pound portfolio, built over ten years from zero, in his longest video.")}
<p>James knows the maths from the inside. He grew a portfolio from nothing to 400,000 pounds across a decade, and his most popular recent video, watched more than 40,000 times, untangles the five April 2027 tax changes his viewers keep getting wrong. His authority on pensions comes from proximity. He has watched compounding work across ten full years, which is exactly the view most non savers have never had.</p>
<p>{qm("krish")} gives the alarm a human face and a deadline. He holds 37,000 pounds in his pension and has set a public target that sounds absurd until he shows the arithmetic, 100,000 pounds before he turns 30, powered by the fact that money invested in your twenties works harder than money invested at any other age.</p>
{qu("krish", "I have got 37,000 pounds in my pension, and I am getting it to 100,000 pounds before I turn 30. Because every one pound I put in now could turn into 32 pounds by the time I retire. Here is why that matters. Time is the only ingredient you cannot buy later, so I am buying as much of it as I can right now.", "https://www.tiktok.com/@krish.kara/video/7665752264383974678", "1,559 views", "His most watched video, seen 34,800 times, warns that waiting for the perfect moment is the most expensive mistake.")}
<p>Krish is building a stocks and shares ISA from zero at 500 pounds a month, on camera, precisely to prove that ordinary earners can do this. The pension target runs alongside it, and the logic is identical in both. Start now, automate, and let decades do what salaries cannot. One pound becoming 32 is not a metaphor. It is compounding at seven percent across forty years, and youth is the only discount code.</p>
<p>{qm("tatiana")} operates at the far end of the same curve and shows where it leads. She holds more than 140,000 pounds in her pension, inside a 400,000 pound net worth built by 33 on a 165,000 pound salary, and she has started teaching pensions from scratch because, as she puts it, the ladies are behind and that needs to change.</p>
{qu("tatiana", "As someone who has over 140,000 pounds invested in my pension and has been doubling down on it this year as an investment vehicle of choice, let us get into the basics of what a pension is and why it is so important that you should have one. A pension is a long term, tax efficient savings pot built up over your working life to fund your retirement. Essentially it is how you pay for your life when you stop working.", "https://www.tiktok.com/@tatianamamixo/video/7693098301448621334", "1,160 views", "Her 400,000 pound net worth reveal, watched 6,858 times with 1,283 likes, names the pension as the quiet engine.")}
<p>Tatiana consolidated four workplace pensions from thirteen years and four employers into a single pot, using PensionBee, and made a video about the three benefits, fewer logins, full visibility, and less paperwork. The admin sounds trivial until you consider that millions of Britons hold scattered pots they never look at, each one bleeding fees and attention. Her consolidation video is pension hygiene made glamorous, and her audience, ambitious women in their late twenties and thirties, is exactly the group the statistics say needs it.</p>
<p>{qm("kafilat")} makes the shortest and sweetest case on the page. In a rapid fire financial planning clip, she names the workplace pension first, before ISAs, before anything, and her reason is a single phrase that every other creator here would endorse.</p>
{qu("kafilat", "Having a financial plan for your future includes staying in your workplace pension, as this is free money that will benefit from compound growth, and opening a stocks and shares ISA and just getting started with whatever you can afford.", "https://www.tiktok.com/@kafilat.finance/video/7675756206878739734", "1,070 views", "Her Save Maxxing series walks viewers through savings systems episode by episode.")}
<p>Free money is doing precise work in that sentence. Employer contributions plus tax relief mean every pound a worker contributes can instantly become two, before markets do anything at all. Opting out of a workplace pension, as millions do, is the only pay cut that employees volunteer for, and Kafilat treats it as the unforced error it is.</p>
<p>{qm("fellas")} shows the monthly machinery at full speed. He invests 1,500 pounds every month, 500 from personal income into a stocks and shares ISA and 1,000 from his limited company into a self invested personal pension, and he has done it every month this year without exception, building toward a portfolio near 140,000 pounds across eight years.</p>
{qu("fellas", "I have invested 1,500 pounds every month this year, and October has been no different. On the first of every month, 500 pounds from my personal income goes into my stocks and shares ISA and 1,000 pounds from my limited company gets invested into my self invested personal pension. Every single penny goes into a boring yet incredibly simple strategy, and that is the whole secret.", "https://www.tiktok.com/@fellasfinance/video/7691678396190821664", "25,300 views", "His Junior ISA video, explaining how a parent's early contributions compound for children, drew 121,700 views.")}
<p>Completing the picture, {qm("emmie")} films the decision most beginners freeze on, choosing the provider. Comparing InvestEngine, AJ Bell, and Interactive Investor for her own self invested personal pension transfer, watched by nearly 7,000 people, she turns provider shopping from a paralysis point into content, asking her audience which they use and whether they would choose it again.</p>
{qu("emmie", "I have decided to open a self invested personal pension and move my existing company pensions into it. I am comparing three providers to find the right fit for me. Which do you use, and would you choose them again? Fees compound too, so the wrapper you pick matters almost as much as what you put inside it.", "https://www.tiktok.com/@emmieedit/video/7688643911379651862", "6,871 views", "Her thematic ETF series and April 2027 tax change explainer round out a catalogue aimed at thinking beginners.")}
<p>Where they agree, and where they split, comes down to priority and plumbing. Everyone agrees the pension comes early, everyone agrees employer money is sacred, everyone agrees compounding needs decades. The split is between the consolidators and the optimisers. Tatiana merged four pots into one for simplicity. Emmie shops three providers for the best fees. Fellas splits salary across ISA and pension by rule. Krish chases a six figure pot by 30 while James waves the national statistics like a warning flag. These are temperament differences, not substantive ones. Beneath them sits a unanimous verdict. The pension is the highest return account most Britons will ever own, because no market anywhere offers an instant doubling of your money on day one, which is what employer matching plus tax relief amounts to.</p>
<p>The takeaway is mechanical. Stay opted into the workplace scheme and claim the full employer match, since declining it is a voluntary pay cut. Consolidate scattered pots so you can see what you own. Raise contributions with every pay rise before lifestyle absorbs it. Supplement with a self invested pension if you are self employed, since 96 percent of the self employed currently face retirement with nothing. Tatiana holds 140,000 pounds. Krish is racing to 100,000. James started from zero and reached 400,000. None of them found a shortcut. They found a form, filled it in, and waited.</p>
"""
check_no_dashes(body3)
open("blog/pension-basics-uk.html", "w").write(page(
 "pension-basics-uk.html",
 "Pension Basics: Free Employer Money, Salary Sacrifice and a 100K by 30 Race",
 "How UK creators think about pensions, from auto-enrolment to early retirement",
 "MONEY ADVICE",
 "Pension Basics: Free Employer Money, Salary Sacrifice and a 100K by 30 Race",
 "How UK creators think about pensions, from auto-enrolment to early retirement",
 ["Pensions"], body3))
